 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $-2a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186326896 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5b6fc54b-cb36-5749-9df6-d5883bd36fd6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326896 #edb/ref 1074 "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}30 & -60 \\\\ -60 & 120\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 30\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -2 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\n Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n-2a+\\frac{b}{c}=-2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{2}{1}=3.\n$$" #edb/ref 1759